---
interface Props { prefix?: string; region?: string }
const { prefix = '', region = 'US' } = Astro.props;
---
<details class="chart-data labor-market-data">
  <summary>Jobs, job seekers and wages</summary>
  <p id={`${prefix}labor-market-summary`}></p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${region} jobs and workforce by year`}>
    <table>
      <caption>Job slots are relative to the initial job count. The four worker groups are shares of the original workforce and add to 100%. Retained workers stay on payroll without filling productive jobs.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Productive job slots</th><th scope="col">In productive jobs</th><th scope="col">Retained on payroll</th><th scope="col">Laid off and seeking</th><th scope="col">Not seeking</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody id={`${prefix}labor-market-table`}></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${region} wage contracts and competition by year`}>
    <table>
      <caption>Contractual wage multipliers relative to the pre-AI rate stay fixed until a worker leaves or is replaced. Real pay still varies with the economy, work effort, taxes and consumer prices. Retention prevents replacement by cheaper hires.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Wage for a new contract</th><th scope="col">Average working contract</th><th scope="col">Incumbents replaced this year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody id={`${prefix}wage-table`}></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small-copy">A share of laid-off workers seeks work. They compete for vacancies and, where layoffs are allowed, existing jobs. Replacing an incumbent does not create another job. Replacement counts are shares of the original workforce. Benefits based on prior income keep using the pre-AI reference.</p>
</details>
